 teams try to get in position for  things they want okay you want something  we want something and the question is  whether to what extent the commissioner  is willing to use the employment of  colin kaepernick as a bargaining chip in  this  tit-for-tat quid pro quo stuff that  happens all the time between league  offices and the teams and if they really  wanted to get it done they get it done  it's a phone call or two maybe three you  get it done you find a team to take him  and people would be surprised to know  that's the way the sausage is made well  that's the way the sausage is made so  look it's one thing to answer a question  that you're asked directly and what else  is he gonna say if you really want him  back in the nfl there's a way to get him  back in the nfl well yeah i agree with  you mike and that's why first off you're  awesome you've been banging the drum  about this the whole time and you know i  actually  i i i refer back to something you you  kind of talked about this weekend i  think it was on pro footballtalk.com  mike where i just found it interesting  and i thought you know nonetheless like  to your point you would think the nfl  could get on a group phone call with all  the owners and say hey we gotta figure  out where we're gonna get colin  kaepernick you know this is good for the  players in our league it's good for our  league in general it's good for society  and it's certainly good for the  african-american society and culture and  everything that's going on in the world  right now so it's a good move but what  i'm amazed about and bringing it back to  what i read over the weekend i think it  was the weekend mike damn i read so many  articles and stuff now i can't even  remember you know  why doesn't anybody want to be branch  ricky that's that's what i don't get  doesn't somebody like you know want to  be that guy that beacon of hey  i made the right move even though  society was scared to do this and i've  done it and you go into the 